# `module.exports = f, module.exports.mark = mark`: the function lies in a variable of the module when the name is stored on it. Nobody has looked at it yet: nothing between the two stores runs code.
# So whoever reads the name finds it, as on an object that is stored to right behind its allocation. One object stands for all closures of a function, so the function has to be made at that one place.
# RUN: valueflow-why values=f0:v10 values=f0:v19 values=f0:v29 values=f0:v37
# stored right behind
# CHECK: values=f0:v10
# CHECK: operand 0 {{*}} 1 members: f5
# a call stands in between
# CHECK: values=f0:v19
# CHECK: operand 0 {{*}} 2 members: primitive0 f5
# another place makes a closure of the function too, and stores nothing on it
# CHECK: values=f0:v29
# CHECK: operand 0 {{*}} 2 members: primitive0 f5
# stored in a later block
# CHECK: values=f0:v37
# CHECK: operand 0 {{*}} 2 members: primitive0 f5

